1
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:05,560
Here is a ball in four-dimensional space.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,270
And here is a cylinder, a little narrower than the ball.

3
00:00:10,210 --> 00:00:14,030
Can we move the ball so that it fits inside?

4
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:17,550
It depends on which moves we allow.

5
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:22,290
With the moves of classical mechanics, the answer is no,

6
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:26,830
and that is one of the founding results of symplectic geometry.

7
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:29,940
The symplectic camel.

8
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:37,030
The four dimensions are phase space for a system with two degrees of freedom.

9
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:44,570
x₁ and x₂ are positions. y₁ and y₂ are the matching momenta.

10
00:00:44,510 --> 00:00:50,200
The symplectic form ω measures a small patch of surface

11
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:56,480
by projecting it to each pair's plane and adding the two signed areas.

12
00:00:56,420 --> 00:01:01,210
Every motion generated by a Hamiltonian preserves ω.

13
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:05,210
Maps that preserve ω are called symplectic.

14
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:13,130
The view shows x₁, y₁ and x₂. Color shows y₂.

15
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:21,060
The cylinder is every point whose (x₁, y₁) lies in a disk of radius R.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,430
So the ball fits exactly when its shadow on the (x₁, y₁) plane fits inside that disk.

17
00:01:28,370 --> 00:01:34,290
The ball has radius r. Its shadow has area πr².

18
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:39,160
The hole has the smaller area πR².

19
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:43,910
First, a map that is not symplectic.

20
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:49,050
Shrink the first plane, and stretch the second by the same factor.

21
00:01:48,990 --> 00:01:52,730
Four-dimensional volume is unchanged.

22
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:58,550
The ball becomes a long cigar and slides right into the cylinder.

23
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:02,110
So volume alone is no obstruction.

24
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,980
Now a symplectic map. We squeeze x₁.

25
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:14,390
Preserving ω forces y₁ to stretch by the same factor,

26
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:17,980
so the shadow becomes an ellipse.

27
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,450
It is narrower, but it is also longer,

28
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:27,380
and its area is still exactly πr². It never fits.

29
00:02:28,630 --> 00:02:35,880
Maybe a linear map is too rigid. So let's try everything at once.

30
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:40,420
Wavy kicks to the momenta, wavy drifts to the positions,

31
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:44,670
rotations that pass material between the two planes,

32
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:48,260
and a squeeze, all running together.

33
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,891
Each piece is exactly symplectic, so the whole flow is too.

34
00:02:53,831 --> 00:02:59,831
The shadow folds and swirls, and its area rises and falls.

35
00:02:59,771 --> 00:03:03,141
It never drops below πr².

36
00:03:04,491 --> 00:03:11,071
This is Mikhail Gromov's non-squeezing theorem, from 1985.

37
00:03:11,011 --> 00:03:16,970
If a ball of radius r fits symplectically in a cylinder of radius R,

38
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:20,141
then r is at most R.

39
00:03:20,081 --> 00:03:24,171
The shadow's area is the right thing to watch.

40
00:03:24,111 --> 00:03:30,471
An area-preserving map of the plane can reshape a shadow into a round disk.

41
00:03:30,411 --> 00:03:35,121
So a shadow smaller than πr² would give a squeeze.

42
00:03:36,371 --> 00:03:45,111
Turn the cylinder so its base is the (x₁, x₂) plane: two positions, no momentum.

43
00:03:45,051 --> 00:03:51,411
ω vanishes on this plane. Planes like this are called Lagrangian.

44
00:03:51,351 --> 00:03:57,491
Now a symplectic map squeezes both positions and stretches both momenta,

45
00:03:57,431 --> 00:04:01,611
and the ball slides through with room to spare.

46
00:04:01,551 --> 00:04:06,491
The obstruction lives only in planes where ω measures area.

47
00:04:07,741 --> 00:04:12,051
So a symplectic map remembers more than volume.

48
00:04:11,991 --> 00:04:17,291
In mechanics, picture a cloud of possible initial states.

49
00:04:17,231 --> 00:04:23,851
No Hamiltonian evolution can shrink its shadow in one position–momentum pair

50
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:29,741
below the starting area, even by pushing the spread into the other pair.

51
00:04:29,681 --> 00:04:33,941
It is a classical echo of the uncertainty principle.

52
00:04:33,881 --> 00:04:37,341
That is why it's called the symplectic camel:

53
00:04:37,281 --> 00:04:41,681
a camel that cannot pass through the eye of a needle.

54
00:04:42,731 --> 00:04:46,381
The interactive version is linked below.

55
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:50,251
Drag the hole, and try to squeeze the ball yourself.
